   "text": "The drawing is a 5\u03b1-pregnane steroid (four fused rings, with an acetyl group on the five-membered ring) and matches ganaxolone, 3\u03b2-methyl-3\u03b1-hydroxy-5\u03b1-pregnan-20-one. I wrote the SMILES from my memory of the ganaxolone SMILES and checked it against the image; I did not derive every centre independently.\n\n**Skeleton:**\n- The five-membered D ring carries an acetyl group (CH3\u2013C(=O)\u2013) on C17, and its C13 carries an angular methyl.\n- The three six-membered rings, A, B and C, are fused to it, and C10 carries a second angular methyl.\n- C3 on ring A carries both an OH and a methyl, so it is a tertiary alcohol.\n\n**Stereochemistry shown by the wedges and hashes:**\n- Wedged (\u03b2): the C17 acetyl, the C13 methyl, the C10 methyl, the C8\u2013H and the C3 methyl.\n- Hashed (\u03b1): the C14\u2013H, the C9\u2013H and the C5\u2013H.\n- The C3 OH is drawn with a plain bond, but it sits opposite the wedged methyl, which gives the 3\u03b1-OH.\n\nI checked two centres against the drawing, C17 and C3. Their handedness agrees with the SMILES. The other centres follow the natural steroid arrangement, which matches the wedges and hashes.\n\nSMILES: CC(=O)[C@H]1CC[C@@H]2[C@@]1(CC[C@H]3[C@H]2CC[C@@H]4[C@@]3(CC[C@](C4)(C)O)C)C"
